unched three hundred  and ten thousand pounds but is it enough  to lift a building given that the Empire  State Building weighs three hundred and  sixty five thousand tons  Kyle calculated that 420 rockets would  be needed just to get the building  airborne the first stage of Saturn's  liftoff consumes 1.4 million pounds of  refined petroleum and 3.17 8 million  pounds of liquid oxygen for 420 rockets  that's this incredibly long number on  your screen right now  pounds worth of chemical weight that  doesn't sound sustainable or good for  the environment  OK ROCK except out so how's about  Columbia's other flotation device  balloon although I think we're gonna  need something a little more heavy-duty  helium floats because at sea level its  density is no point two kilograms per  cubic meter whereas the density of air  is 1.2 kilograms per cubic meter max  says a cubic meter of helium can lift  one kilogram of mass fill plant who  writes slates bad astronomy blog  crunched the numbers on how much helium  would be needed to raise the Empire  State Building  we know the Empire State weighs 365,000  tons which works out as 330 million  kilograms do the math and that 330  million cubic meters of helium Kyle Hill  noted that this is the same amount of  helium that could power 1650  Hindenburg's and Phil worked out that  you would need a balloon with the radius  of 430 meters to fix it all in bearing  in mind it's 443 meters from the ground  to the very tip of the Empire State  Building so that's a pretty pretty big  balloon so while technically possible  the best kind of possible this sounds  like another no-go I'm running out of  ideas here so let's try quantum physics  don't turn off it's genuinely quite  interesting a superconductor is a  material with two very important  properties number one it displays zero  electrical resistance which means that  electricity can flow through it with no  loss of current and number two  superconductor rejects magnetic fields  so if y